RD('C2','The Problem of Moral Luck','Vấn đề may rủi đạo đức','🎲','Consider two motorists who drive home after an identical evening, each slightly the worse for drink and each equally cavalier about it. One reaches her door without incident; the other, through no fault of her own, encounters a child who darts into the road. We condemn the second driver far more severely, yet nothing in her character or deliberation distinguished her from the first. This is the puzzle that Thomas Nagel and Bernard Williams bequeathed to moral philosophy: if responsibility ought to track only what lies within our control, then our judgements seem scandalously hostage to fortune. The Kantian ideal of a will that shines like a jewel, untouched by consequences, collides with the plain fact that we do not, and perhaps cannot, assess people in so antiseptic a manner. Some philosophers bite the bullet and declare our reactive attitudes irrational; others contend that the very notion of an agent insulated from circumstance is a fiction, since character itself is forged by upbringing and happenstance. What is conspicuous is that neither camp can fully inhabit its own position. The consistent Kantian still feels reproach, and the consistent consequentialist still feels the need to apologise for her good intentions.','Hai người lái xe say rượu như nhau nhưng chỉ một người gây tai nạn, và triết học đạo đức chưa giải thích được vì sao ta phán xét họ khác nhau.','What distinguishes the two drivers in the opening scenario?|*One of them happens to meet a child in the road|One of them is far more intoxicated|One of them deliberates more carefully§What can be inferred about the author\'s view of the two philosophical camps?|The Kantians have decisively refuted their rivals|Both camps have solved the puzzle|*Neither can consistently live by its own stance§The phrase "bite the bullet" is used to describe philosophers who|attempt to avoid the problem altogether|*accept an uncomfortable conclusion for the sake of consistency|rely on violent metaphors to make a point');
RD('C2','Gettier and the Fragility of Knowledge','Gettier và sự mong manh của tri thức','🧩','For the better part of two millennia, philosophers were content to define knowledge as justified true belief. Then, in 1963, Edmund Gettier published a paper of scarcely three pages that unsettled the consensus. His counterexamples described subjects who hold a belief on excellent grounds, whose belief happens to be true, and yet who could hardly be said to know. A stopped clock, glanced at on the one occasion each day when it displays the correct time, yields exactly such a case: the conclusion is sound, the reasoning respectable, and the correctness entirely fortuitous. The ensuing decades produced an almost baroque proliferation of amendments, requiring that justification not rest on false premises, or that belief track the truth across nearby possibilities, or that it issue from a reliable faculty. Each patch invited fresh counterexamples, so that the literature began to resemble an arms race between ingenuity and ingenuity. Some epistemologists, weary of the contest, now suspect that knowledge is not analysable at all, and propose instead to treat it as a primitive notion from which belief and justification are derived rather than the reverse. Whether that manoeuvre is a profound reorientation or a polite surrender remains, characteristically, disputed.','Bài báo ngắn của Gettier năm 1963 phá vỡ định nghĩa tri thức là niềm tin đúng được biện minh, và nhiều nỗ lực sửa chữa vẫn chưa thành công.','What does the stopped clock example illustrate?|A belief that is both false and unjustified|*A true, justified belief that is true only by coincidence|A reliable faculty that produces knowledge§Why does the author compare the literature to an arms race?|*Each proposed fix was met by a new counterexample|Philosophers deliberately sabotaged each other|The debate attracted military funding§The author\'s attitude to treating knowledge as a primitive notion is best described as|wholeheartedly enthusiastic|openly contemptuous|*undecided about whether it is insight or capitulation');
RD('C2','Art for Art\'s Sake','Nghệ thuật vì nghệ thuật','🖼️','The slogan l\'art pour l\'art, imported from French salons into Victorian drawing rooms, was never quite the doctrine of frivolity that its detractors imagined. Its proponents, among them Théophile Gautier and, in a more sardonic register, Oscar Wilde, insisted that a work of art answers to no tribunal but its own: it need not instruct, console or improve, and to demand that it do so is to mistake a painting for a sermon. Yet the position is subtler than mere disdain for the public. To claim autonomy for art is also to claim a kind of dignity for it, a refusal to let aesthetic experience be conscripted as an instrument of commerce, piety or propaganda. The irony, of course, is that the stance generated its own orthodoxy. Aesthetes who sneered at moralists soon policed taste with a severity that any censor might have envied, and the cult of pure form proved quite compatible with snobbery of the most exclusive kind. Perhaps the enduring lesson is that autonomy and indifference are different things. A work may owe nothing to utility and still be bound, by the sheer pressure of its making, to say something about the world that produced it.','Chủ nghĩa nghệ thuật vì nghệ thuật khẳng định tính tự trị của nghệ thuật, nhưng tự nó cũng sinh ra giáo điều và thói trưởng giả.','According to the passage, what did proponents of the slogan chiefly reject?|The idea that art can be beautiful|The importance of artistic skill|*The demand that art serve instruction or utility§What irony does the author identify?|Moralists became aesthetes|*Aesthetes enforced taste as strictly as censors|Wilde disliked French salons§The final sentence suggests that artistic autonomy|*does not exempt a work from reflecting its world|requires artists to ignore their society|is a refuge for commercial interests');
RD('C2','The Long Take and the Ethics of Looking','Cú máy dài và đạo đức của cái nhìn','🎬','Few cinematic techniques provoke such devotion as the long take, in which a scene unfolds without a cut and the viewer is denied the relief of editorial punctuation. André Bazin, writing in the aftermath of the war, argued that the sustained shot preserved the ambiguity of reality, leaving spectators free to survey the frame and form their own conclusions rather than being herded towards a meaning by montage. There is an ethical claim buried in this aesthetic preference: that to cut is to decide on our behalf what matters, whereas to hold is to trust. Sceptics retort that the long take is hardly innocent. The director who stages a ten-minute sequence has choreographed every gesture within it, and the apparent liberty of the gaze is carefully rationed. Moreover, duration can be a form of coercion, compelling us to witness what we might prefer to avert our eyes from. The best practitioners seem to understand this tension and exploit it, using the unbroken shot to implicate the audience in what it watches. Patience, in such films, ceases to be a virtue we bring to the cinema and becomes a burden the cinema places upon us.','Cú máy dài được ca ngợi vì trao tự do cho người xem, nhưng cũng có thể là sự ép buộc tinh vi khi bắt ta phải nhìn.','According to Bazin, what is the merit of the sustained shot?|*It preserves the ambiguity of reality for the viewer|It hides the director\'s intentions entirely|It makes editing unnecessary in all films§Why do sceptics consider the long take "hardly innocent"?|It is too expensive to film|Audiences tend to dislike it|*Every gesture has been carefully staged by the director§The final sentence suggests that in some films, patience is|a skill that directors must acquire|*a demand imposed on the viewer rather than a quality they offer|a technique reserved for documentary');
RD('C2','Untranslatable Words and the Translator\'s Dilemma','Từ không thể dịch và nan đề của người dịch','📖','Every translator eventually meets the word that will not travel. The Portuguese saudade, the German Weltschmerz, the Japanese komorebi, the sunlight filtering through leaves: such terms are routinely paraded as proof that languages carve experience at different joints. The claim is seductive but needs qualification. Nearly any word can be rendered by a paraphrase; what resists transfer is not the meaning but the economy, the way a single syllable compresses what an English sentence must unpack at length. Literary translation therefore resembles less a transaction than a negotiation in which something is invariably forfeited. The translator may be faithful to sense and sacrifice music, or preserve rhythm at the expense of idiom, or retain the strangeness of the original and risk alienating readers who wanted fluency. Venuti has famously berated the Anglophone tradition for domesticating foreign texts until they read as though they had been written in English, effacing the translator and the otherness alike. His critics reply that readers are not obliged to relish difficulty on principle. The wise translator, one suspects, accepts that there is no perfect solution, only a sequence of defensible losses, and takes some consolation in the fact that a translation, unlike a treaty, is never signed.','Dịch văn học luôn đòi hỏi đánh đổi giữa nghĩa, âm điệu và sự lạ lẫm, và không có giải pháp hoàn hảo.','According to the passage, what actually resists translation?|The basic meaning of the word|*The compact economy with which a word expresses meaning|The grammar of the source language§What can be inferred about Venuti\'s critics?|*They think readers should not have to enjoy difficulty by default|They deny that translators exist|They prefer literal translation above all§The comparison with a treaty suggests that translations are|legally binding documents|inevitably hostile to the original|*always open to revision and never final');
RD('C2','Grimm\'s Law and the Archaeology of Sound','Định luật Grimm và khảo cổ học của âm thanh','🔊','That the English word father and the Latin pater should be cousins is hardly obvious to the casual ear. Yet in the early nineteenth century, Jacob Grimm, better remembered for his collaboration with his brother on fairy tales, formalised a regularity that made the kinship demonstrable. The Indo-European stops, he observed, shifted systematically in the Germanic branch: a p in Latin or Greek tends to correspond to an f in English, a t to a th, a k to an h. Hence pater and father, tres and three, cornu and horn. What startled the philologists was not the individual correspondences but their regularity, which suggested that sound change obeys laws rather than whims. The Neogrammarians later sharpened the claim into the dictum that sound laws admit no exceptions, and apparent exceptions, such as those resolved by Verner, were taken as invitations to find the hidden condition rather than as refutations. The achievement was as much methodological as descriptive. It taught linguists that a vanished language can be reconstructed by comparison, and that the apparent chaos of vocabulary conceals a buried order that patient reasoning can retrieve.','Định luật Grimm cho thấy các âm thay đổi theo quy luật, mở đường cho việc tái dựng các ngôn ngữ đã mất bằng phương pháp so sánh.','What did the philologists find most surprising about Grimm\'s findings?|The existence of fairy tales|The similarity of Latin and Greek|*The regularity of the correspondences§How did the Neogrammarians treat apparent exceptions?|They discarded the whole theory|*They searched for an underlying condition explaining them|They declared them to be mistakes in the records§The passage implies the main legacy of Grimm\'s work was|*a method for reconstructing lost languages|a collection of folk tales|a ban on borrowing words');
RD('C2','Marbury and the Birth of Judicial Review','Marbury và sự ra đời của quyền rà soát tư pháp','⚖️','The Constitution of the United States nowhere states that the courts may strike down an act of Congress, a fact that has not prevented the practice from becoming the signature feature of American public law. Its origin is conventionally traced to Marbury v. Madison, decided in 1803, in which Chief Justice John Marshall contrived a judgement of remarkable political dexterity. Declining to order the new administration to deliver a commission, he thereby avoided a confrontation the Court could not have won, while simultaneously declaring that a statute repugnant to the Constitution is void and that it is emphatically the province of the judiciary to say what the law is. Critics have long observed that the decision conferred power on the Court by denying it a lesser one, a stratagem worthy of a chess master. Defenders reply that the logic of a written constitution, which binds the legislature, inevitably requires someone to enforce the limits. The debate has never ended: if unelected judges may overrule the representatives of the people, by what authority do they do so, and who guards against their errors? Marshall left the question not so much answered as institutionalised.','Phán quyết Marbury v. Madison năm 1803 xác lập quyền của tòa án hủy bỏ luật vi hiến, một quyền không có trong văn bản hiến pháp.','What did Marshall avoid by declining to order delivery of the commission?|*A confrontation the Court was unlikely to win|A conflict with Congress over a statute|A rewriting of the Constitution§What does the author mean by the decision being "a stratagem worthy of a chess master"?|It was a game played for entertainment|It was copied from European courts|*It gained greater power through an apparent concession§The final sentence suggests that Marshall|answered every objection to judicial review|*established the practice without resolving its legitimacy|abolished the power of Congress');
RD('C2','Chokepoints and the Geography of Leverage','Các điểm nghẽn và địa lý của đòn bẩy','🌊','Maps flatter our sense of an unbounded world, but commerce flows through a handful of narrow passages that no amount of rhetoric about globalisation has widened. The Strait of Hormuz, the Strait of Malacca and the Suez Canal each funnel a conspicuous share of the planet\'s energy and merchandise through channels that a modest naval force could, in principle, obstruct. The strategic implication is paradoxical. Dependence on such corridors confers power on whoever commands their shores, yet the user of that power is constrained, because closing a strait wounds the closer almost as grievously as the closed. Hence the peculiar equilibrium in which threats are frequent and execution is rare. Planners nonetheless cannot be sanguine. A single grounded vessel in 2021 was enough to paralyse the Suez Canal for six days and to remind a complacent public how little slack there is in the system. Alternative routes, pipelines and stockpiles mitigate the danger without abolishing it, and each carries costs and vulnerabilities of its own. To think geopolitically, then, is to recognise that the elegance of just-in-time supply chains rests upon the continued forbearance of geography and of the powers that border it.','Một vài eo biển hẹp chi phối thương mại toàn cầu, tạo ra đòn bẩy chiến lược nhưng cũng làm hệ thống dễ tổn thương.','Why are threats to close a strait seldom carried out?|They are prohibited by treaty|*Closure would harm the closer almost as much|Alternative routes are always cheaper§What does the 2021 incident suggest?|*The global supply system has little slack|Ships are rarely a danger to canals|Naval forces can easily reopen channels§The tone of the phrase "a complacent public" is|admiring|neutral and technical|*mildly critical');
RD('C2','The Zero Lower Bound','Giới hạn lãi suất không','🏦','For decades, central bankers behaved as though the interest rate were an instrument of limitless finesse: lower it to stimulate, raise it to cool. The financial crisis of 2008 exposed the flaw in this serenity. With rates already pressed close to zero, the authorities discovered that a nominal rate cannot be pushed much below nought without inducing depositors to hoard banknotes, and that the economy may still require far more stimulus than a rate of nought can deliver. Economists call this predicament the zero lower bound, and it transformed monetary policy from an exercise in calibration into an exercise in improvisation. Quantitative easing, forward guidance and, in a few jurisdictions, mildly negative rates were deployed with a mixture of ingenuity and trepidation. Their efficacy is still contested: sceptics point out that asset purchases enriched the holders of assets, while enthusiasts credit them with averting a second depression. What is beyond dispute is that the episode undermined the presumption that monetary policy alone can steer the economy. Fiscal authorities, long treated as clumsy interlopers, were once again invited to the table, and the old Keynesian insight that spending can fill a gap which cheap money cannot was rehabilitated with a certain sheepishness.','Khi lãi suất chạm gần mức không, ngân hàng trung ương phải ứng biến, và vai trò của chính sách tài khóa được xem xét lại.','Why can nominal rates not fall far below zero?|Banks forbid it by law|Inflation would immediately vanish|*Depositors would hold cash instead§What is implied about fiscal policy after 2008?|It was permanently abolished|*Its value was acknowledged again|It was proven to be worthless§The word "sheepishness" suggests that the rehabilitation of Keynesian ideas was done|*with some embarrassment|with great enthusiasm|in complete secrecy');
RD('C2','Epigenetics and the Inheritance of Experience','Di truyền ngoại gen và sự thừa kế kinh nghiệm','🧬','The genome is often likened to a blueprint, a metaphor that suggests an architect\'s fixed intention. Epigenetics unsettles the analogy by revealing a layer of chemical annotation, such as methyl groups attached to DNA, that governs which genes are read and which remain mute without altering the underlying sequence. Because these marks respond to environment, diet and stress, they furnish a plausible mechanism by which experience might leave a durable trace on the organism. Much more contentious is the suggestion that such traces can be transmitted across generations, a notion that tempts the unwary into resurrecting Lamarck. Studies of rodents offer intriguing hints, and observational work on populations that endured famine has been invoked as human corroboration, yet the confounders are formidable, and the epigenetic slate is largely wiped clean in the earliest stages of development. Prudence therefore counsels against the breathless headlines that announce that grandparents\' trauma is written into their descendants\' cells. The more defensible claim is subtler: that the relationship between gene and circumstance is a conversation, not a decree, and that biology has never been quite as deterministic as popular imagination, or occasionally popular science, has supposed.','Di truyền ngoại gen cho thấy môi trường ảnh hưởng đến cách gen biểu hiện, nhưng việc truyền qua nhiều thế hệ vẫn còn gây tranh cãi.','What do epigenetic marks regulate?|*Which genes are expressed|The order of the DNA sequence|The number of chromosomes§Why does the author advise caution about transgenerational claims?|Rodent studies have been proved fraudulent|Famine has never affected populations|*Confounding factors are strong and marks are largely reset early on§The author\'s conclusion presents the gene-environment relationship as|a fixed decree|*an ongoing conversation|an unresolved war');
RD('C2','The Hard Problem of Consciousness','Vấn đề khó của ý thức','🧠','Neuroscience has made undeniable progress in explaining how the brain discriminates stimuli, integrates information and governs behaviour. David Chalmers has argued, however, that these accomplishments address only the easy problems, easy in the technical sense that we know what a solution would look like, namely a mechanism. The hard problem is why any of this processing should be accompanied by felt experience at all. One can imagine, without apparent contradiction, a creature that behaves exactly as we do and yet harbours no inner life, a philosophical zombie. If such a being is conceivable, the thought runs, then physical description alone cannot entail consciousness. Physicalists have responded in several ways. Some deny the coherence of the zombie; others, like Daniel Dennett, contend that the hard problem is an artefact of a misleading introspective illusion, and that once the easy problems are solved nothing will remain to be explained. To the sceptic this smacks of explaining something by explaining it away. The disagreement is rendered peculiarly stubborn by the absence of any agreed criterion for what would count as progress. Each side regards the other\'s intuitions as the data to be dismissed, and neither is able to produce the decisive experiment that would force a concession.','Vấn đề khó hỏi vì sao xử lý thông tin lại đi kèm trải nghiệm chủ quan, và các trường phái vẫn bất đồng gay gắt.','Why does Chalmers call certain problems "easy"?|They require no scientific effort|*We can envisage a mechanistic solution to them|They have already been solved completely§How might Dennett be characterised, according to the passage?|*As treating the hard problem as an illusion|As accepting the zombie argument|As abandoning physicalism§Why is the disagreement described as stubborn?|Neither side has studied the brain|Experiments have conclusively settled it|*There is no shared standard of what counts as progress');
RD('C2','The Horizon Problem and Cosmic Inflation','Vấn đề chân trời và lạm phát vũ trụ','🌌','The cosmic microwave background is astonishingly uniform: wherever one points an antenna, the temperature of this relic radiation is the same to within a few parts in a hundred thousand. This is puzzling because, on the standard picture of an expanding universe, regions lying on opposite sides of the sky could never have exchanged light, let alone reached thermal equilibrium; they were, so to speak, strangers who somehow dressed identically. In the early 1980s, Alan Guth proposed a resolution of striking boldness. Suppose that, in the first fraction of a second, space expanded exponentially, so that a minuscule patch that had already equilibrated was stretched to encompass everything we can now observe. Inflation would also explain why space appears so nearly flat and would convert quantum jitters into the seeds of galaxies. The theory has been celebrated for its explanatory economy, though not without reservations. The physical field that drove the expansion has never been identified, and some cosmologists complain that inflation, in many of its versions, generates a multiplicity of universes and thereby forfeits testable predictions. A theory that explains so much so elegantly may be right, but elegance alone has never been a licence to declare a question closed.','Lạm phát vũ trụ giải thích vì sao bức xạ nền vi sóng đồng đều đến vậy, nhưng vẫn còn nhiều dè dặt về bằng chứng.','What puzzle does the uniform background radiation present?|Its temperature varies wildly|It is too weak to measure|*Distant regions could not have reached equilibrium by exchanging light§What does inflation propose?|The universe contracted at first|*A tiny equilibrated patch was stretched enormously|Light travelled faster after the first second§The final sentence suggests that the author believes|*elegance does not prove a theory true|inflation has been refuted|cosmologists should abandon the question');
RD('C2','The Tragedy of the Atmospheric Commons','Bi kịch của bầu khí quyển chung','🌍','Garrett Hardin\'s parable of the common pasture, in which each herdsman profits from adding one more animal while the cost of overgrazing is shared by all, has acquired a melancholy topicality. The atmosphere is the ultimate commons: no one owns it, everyone uses it as a dump for carbon, and the harm from any single emission is diffused so thinly that restraint appears pointless to the individual. International climate governance is an attempt to overcome this predicament without a sovereign above the nations. The Paris Agreement of 2015 embodies the compromise reached: rather than binding targets imposed from above, it relies on pledges that states devise for themselves, subject to periodic review and the pressure of reputation. Its architects reasoned that a modest agreement that everyone joins is preferable to a stringent one that the powerful decline to sign. Critics counter that voluntary pledges invite free-riding and that the sum of national ambitions falls well short of what the science demands. Elinor Ostrom, to be fair, showed that communities frequently manage shared resources without either privatisation or coercion, but the conditions she identified, trust, monitoring and graduated sanctions, are exactly what a planet of rivals finds hardest to assemble.','Khí hậu là tài sản chung khó quản lý, và các cam kết tự nguyện kiểu Paris vừa khả thi vừa chưa đủ.','Why does restraint seem pointless to individual emitters?|*The harm of one emission is spread thinly across everyone|The atmosphere belongs to a single owner|Emissions are always beneficial§What reasoning lay behind the Paris Agreement\'s design?|A stringent treaty is always easily enforced|Reputation has no influence on states|*A widely joined modest accord is better than a strict one few sign§The author\'s final point about Ostrom implies that|her research was mistaken|*her conditions are difficult to achieve globally|communities cannot manage resources');
RD('C2','Who Audits the Algorithm?','Ai kiểm toán thuật toán?','🤖','When a loan is refused or a parole application rejected, the applicant is entitled in principle to ask why. When the decision is rendered by a statistical model trained on millions of past cases, the answer may be that nobody can say, not even the engineers who built it. This opacity is not merely an inconvenience; it corrodes the possibility of contestation, which is the foundation of procedural fairness. Defenders of automated systems rightly observe that human decision-makers are hardly paragons of consistency, and that a model, unlike a magistrate, does not become irritable before lunch. Yet this argument evades the central difficulty. A biased judge can be named, challenged and replaced, whereas a bias embedded in training data may masquerade as objectivity precisely because it wears the livery of mathematics. Proposals for algorithmic audits, impact assessments and rights to explanation are therefore best understood as attempts to restore a measure of accountability that technology has quietly displaced. Whether such measures can be anything more than ritual depends on whether auditors are independent, whether they obtain access to the systems they review, and whether anybody is willing to pay the price, in speed or profit, of discovering unwelcome results.','Quyết định của thuật toán thiếu minh bạch làm suy yếu khả năng phản biện, nên cần kiểm toán độc lập có thực quyền.','Why does opacity undermine fairness, according to the passage?|It makes decisions slower|*It prevents people from contesting decisions|It reduces the amount of data available§What does "wears the livery of mathematics" imply?|*Bias can look objective because it is dressed in numbers|Mathematicians wear uniforms|Algorithms are always accurate§The author is sceptical that audits will work unless|auditors are employed by the developers|the systems are made faster|*auditors are independent and have access');
RD('C2','Ethos, Pathos and the Sceptical Audience','Ethos, pathos và thính giả hoài nghi','🎤','Aristotle\'s tripartite scheme of persuasion, appeal to the speaker\'s character, to the audience\'s emotions and to the argument itself, has endured because it describes what orators actually do rather than what logicians wish they would. Modern audiences, however, have been inoculated by a century of advertising and political theatre, and the unadorned appeal to feeling now tends to provoke suspicion rather than assent. The most accomplished speakers have adapted. They understand that credibility is not asserted but accrued, that a concession made early in an address purchases a hearing for the claims that follow, and that the plain admission of uncertainty can be more persuasive than a show of omniscience. Lincoln\'s brevity at Gettysburg, in which a few hundred words eclipsed the two hours of oratory that preceded them, is often cited as a model, though one may suspect that its power owed as much to the occasion as to economy. What endures from the classical rhetoricians is the insight that persuasion is a relationship, not a performance. The speaker who regards the audience as an obstacle to be overcome will generally be found out; the one who regards it as a jury to be respected may, with luck, be believed.','Hùng biện hiện đại cần sự tin cậy và khiêm tốn, vì thính giả quen với quảng cáo nên nghi ngờ lối kích động cảm xúc.','Why has the unadorned emotional appeal become less effective?|Aristotle rejected emotion|Speeches have become much longer|*Audiences have grown wary through exposure to advertising and political theatre§According to the passage, what does an early concession accomplish?|It weakens the whole speech|*It earns a hearing for later claims|It replaces the need for argument§The author concludes that persuasion is chiefly|*a relationship of respect|a one-sided performance|a matter of volume');
RD('C2','The Loneliness of Command','Nỗi cô độc của quyền chỉ huy','👑','Every institution flatters its leaders with the illusion of proximity. They are surrounded by colleagues, briefed by advisers and applauded at conferences, yet those who have held high office frequently describe a solitude that no subordinate can fully appreciate. The explanation is structural rather than psychological. A leader\'s decisions are weighed against alternatives that remain forever unobserved, so that the costs of a choice are borne in public while the counterfactual benefits go unrecorded. Moreover, candour is a casualty of rank: those who depend on a superior\'s favour will tend to soften unwelcome truths until the superior is advised, with great courtesy, that all is well. The wiser executives therefore cultivate what might be called institutionalised dissent, appointing sceptics, soliciting criticism from the periphery and distrusting unanimity as a symptom rather than a comfort. Even so, the final act of judgement cannot be delegated, and it is here that responsibility acquires its distinctive weight. A leader may consult widely, but the signature at the foot of the page is singular. Perhaps the mark of genuine authority is not the absence of doubt but the capacity to act while retaining it, and to bear the consequence without demanding that others share the burden.','Người lãnh đạo thường cô độc vì thiếu phản hồi thẳng thắn và vì quyết định cuối cùng không thể ủy thác.','Why is candour described as a "casualty of rank"?|*Subordinates soften bad news to stay in favour|Leaders prefer silence|Advisers lack information§What do wise executives do about the problem?|They avoid consulting anyone|They trust unanimity above all|*They deliberately seek out dissent§The phrase "the signature at the foot of the page is singular" suggests that|leaders must sign more documents|*final responsibility rests with one person|decisions are always made unanimously');
RD('C2','Sisyphus and the Uses of Absurdity','Sisyphus và công dụng của sự phi lý','🪨','Camus ended his essay on the myth of Sisyphus with a sentence that has been quoted to the point of embalmment: one must imagine Sisyphus happy. It is easy to treat this as bravado, the cheerful grimace of a man condemned to roll a boulder uphill for eternity. But the argument is subtler. The absurd, for Camus, arises from the collision between our hunger for meaning and the silence of the universe, and the available responses are limited. One may seek escape in religious faith, which he regarded as a leap of intellectual abdication; one may choose suicide, which concedes the contest; or one may persist in full awareness that the demand for ultimate justification will never be met. Sisyphus, conscious of his predicament and scornful of it, is superior to his fate precisely because he does not pretend that it is something else. His happiness is not a reward but an attitude, a refusal to let the absence of cosmic purpose empty the moment of its worth. Whether this stance is heroic or merely a sophisticated consolation is a question each reader answers according to temperament, but it is difficult to deny that it endows ordinary labour with a peculiar dignity.','Camus cho rằng ta có thể sống đủ đầy trong sự phi lý bằng cách nhìn thẳng vào nó thay vì trốn tránh.','According to Camus, what produces the absurd?|The brevity of human life alone|*The clash between our need for meaning and the universe\'s silence|The cruelty of the gods§Why is Sisyphus considered superior to his fate?|*He faces it lucidly without pretending otherwise|He eventually escapes from it|He believes the boulder will stay at the top§The author\'s final assessment of Camus\'s stance is|entirely dismissive|a confident refutation|*open-ended, leaving judgement to the reader');
RD('C2','The Lamp at Ardnamurchan Point','Ngọn đèn ở mũi Ardnamurchan','🏮','Old Maclaren had kept the light for thirty-one winters, and in all that time he had never once been thanked by anyone who understood what he did. The ships that passed were grateful only in the manner of men who do not know they have been spared; the Board in Edinburgh acknowledged him in the dry dialect of expenditure. He did not mind. There was a discipline in the work that suited him, a daily liturgy of brass and paraffin and glass, and he had come to believe that vigilance was a kind of prayer that required no listener. On the night the storm arrived, however, he found himself unaccountably afraid, not of the gale, which he had known in every mood, but of the thought that the light might fail on the one night it was truly needed, and that its long reliability would be revealed as nothing but luck. He climbed the stair slowly, his hand upon the cold rail, and observed that the flame was steady. It occurred to him, with the dispassion of the very tired, that this was all there was to courage: to climb the stair and attend to the flame, while the sea, indifferent and magnificent, did precisely what it had always done.','Người gác đèn biển già nhận ra rằng can đảm chỉ là tiếp tục làm bổn phận một cách bền bỉ trước biển cả dửng dưng.','How do the passing ships regard the keeper?|They thank him openly each year|They despise his isolation|*They are grateful without knowing it§What frightens Maclaren on the night of the storm?|The violence of the gale|*That the light might fail when most needed|That the Board will dismiss him§The final sentence suggests that Maclaren views courage as|*quiet persistence in duty|a defiance of the sea|a reward for long service');
RD('C2','Entrenchment and the Dead Hand','Điều khoản bất khả thay đổi và bàn tay người chết','📜','Constitutions are, by design, difficult to amend, and the difficulty is not an accident of drafting but the whole point. Entrenchment places certain commitments, such as the separation of powers or the protection of speech, beyond the reach of transient majorities, on the theory that the passions of a season should not be permitted to dismantle the settled principles of a generation. Yet the arrangement invites an old objection, usually attributed to Jefferson in one form or another: why should the living be governed by the dead? A people forbidden to revise its fundamental law may find itself bound by the anxieties of eighteenth-century gentlemen, and the more rigid the text, the more elaborate the interpretive contortions needed to accommodate circumstances its authors could not have conceived. Some jurisdictions have therefore adopted staggered procedures, permitting ordinary amendment of minor provisions while demanding extraordinary consensus for the foundations. Others entrust the courts with the task of fashioning a living tradition. Neither solution is without cost. Flexibility erodes the very security that entrenchment was meant to supply, while rigidity risks provoking, when pressure finally becomes irresistible, not reform but rupture.','Hiến pháp khó sửa nhằm bảo vệ nguyên tắc nền tảng, nhưng sự cứng nhắc có thể buộc người sống chịu ràng buộc bởi người đã khuất.','What is the purpose of entrenchment, according to the passage?|*To shield core principles from temporary majorities|To make constitutions easier to amend|To give courts total authority§What risk does excessive rigidity pose?|Citizens will lose interest in politics|Courts will refuse to interpret the text|*Pressure may eventually lead to rupture rather than reform§The phrase "the dead hand" in the title alludes to|the physical remains of constitutional drafters|*the influence of past generations on the present|a punishment imposed by courts');
RD('C2','Language and the Shape of Thought','Ngôn ngữ và hình hài của tư tưởng','🗣️','The strong version of the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, that the language we speak imprisons us within a particular conception of reality, has been largely abandoned, though not without leaving a lingering fascination. Careful experiments have found no evidence that speakers of languages lacking a future tense are incapable of planning, nor that those with few colour terms are blind to the spectrum. What survives is a more modest and arguably more interesting claim: that habitual patterns of expression can nudge attention and memory in subtle, measurable ways. Speakers of languages that mark spatial relations by compass direction rather than by left and right, for example, appear to maintain an extraordinary sense of orientation, and those whose grammar demands that a speaker indicate the source of his knowledge may be more alert to the question of evidence. The difficulty lies in separating influence from correlation, since culture and language travel together and neither can be cleanly subtracted. It is wise, therefore, to resist both extremes: the romantic notion that each tongue is a sealed world, and the dismissive one that grammar is merely a clothing that leaves the body of thought unaffected. Language, it seems, is less a cage than a groove along which attention tends to run.','Giả thuyết mạnh Sapir-Whorf đã bị bác bỏ, nhưng ngôn ngữ vẫn có thể định hướng sự chú ý và trí nhớ một cách tinh tế.','What do careful experiments indicate about the strong hypothesis?|It has been confirmed repeatedly|*It lacks supporting evidence|It applies only to colour terms§Why is it hard to establish influence of language on thought?|*Language and culture are intertwined and cannot be separated cleanly|Few languages are available to study|Speakers refuse to take part§The metaphor of a "groove" suggests language|permanently locks thought in place|has no effect on thought|*guides attention without absolutely confining it');
RD('C2','Editing the Germline','Chỉnh sửa dòng mầm','✂️','Few technologies have outpaced the moral vocabulary available to evaluate them as swiftly as CRISPR. The distinction between somatic editing, which alters the cells of a single patient, and germline editing, which alters embryos and thereby every subsequent generation, is generally regarded as a threshold. Somatic therapies raise the familiar questions of safety and consent; germline interventions raise the unfamiliar question of consent on behalf of those who do not yet exist and cannot refuse. When a researcher announced in 2018 that he had produced edited twins, the scientific community responded with near-unanimous condemnation, less because the thing was inconceivable than because it was premature, secretive and procedurally reckless. Yet the episode exposed a deeper unease. If the prevention of a grave hereditary disease is permissible, on what principled ground does one forbid the enhancement of ordinary traits, given that the boundary between therapy and improvement is notoriously porous? Advocates of caution invoke the slippery slope, which is frequently a fallacy but occasionally a description. A more productive stance may be to ask not whether the technology is natural, a category that has never settled a moral argument, but who would bear its risks, who would enjoy its benefits, and who would be given a voice in deciding.','Chỉnh sửa dòng mầm đặt ra câu hỏi đạo đức về sự đồng ý của thế hệ chưa sinh và ranh giới mong manh giữa trị liệu và nâng cao.','Why is germline editing considered a threshold?|It is cheaper than somatic therapy|It is performed only on adults|*Its changes pass to future generations§Why was the 2018 announcement condemned?|The science was judged impossible|*It was premature, secretive and procedurally reckless|The twins were visibly harmed§The author\'s remark on the slippery slope is|*balanced, noting it is sometimes a fallacy and sometimes apt|entirely dismissive|enthusiastically in favour');
RD('C2','The Seduction of Polygenic Scores','Sức hấp dẫn của điểm số đa gen','📊','A polygenic score condenses the small effects of thousands of genetic variants into a single number that predicts, probabilistically, the risk of a condition or the likelihood of a trait. In medicine the technique holds real promise, since it may identify individuals at elevated risk of heart disease long before symptoms appear. But numbers possess a seductive authority that frequently exceeds their warrant. The scores are derived chiefly from studies of people of European ancestry and perform markedly worse in other populations, a bias that threatens to widen rather than narrow disparities in care. Moreover, prediction at the level of a population is not prediction at the level of the person: a score may shift the odds modestly while leaving the individual outcome overwhelmingly dependent on circumstance. The commercial temptation is to sell such figures as destiny, and the more speculative applications, such as the ranking of embryos by projected educational attainment, rest on correlations that conflate genetic effects with the advantages that wealthy families confer on their children. None of this impugns the science itself, which is advancing rapidly. It merely reminds us that a probability is not a verdict, and that those who traffic in them bear a special duty to state their limits as plainly as their promise.','Điểm số đa gen hứa hẹn trong y học nhưng thiên lệch dân số và dễ bị thổi phồng thành định mệnh.','Why do polygenic scores perform worse in some populations?|*They are mostly based on studies of people of European ancestry|Genes differ in how many variants they have|The technique is illegal elsewhere§What problem affects ranking embryos by educational attainment?|Embryos cannot be sequenced|Education is unrelated to any trait|*The correlations mix genetic effects with environmental advantage§The final sentence of the passage conveys a tone of|cynical dismissal|*cautious responsibility|uncritical excitement');
RD('C2','The Ship of Theseus and the Self','Con tàu của Theseus và cái tôi','⛵','The ancient puzzle asks whether a ship whose planks have been replaced one by one remains the same vessel, and whether it would be so if the discarded planks were reassembled elsewhere. As a riddle about timber it is merely amusing; as a riddle about persons it becomes disquieting. Our cells are renewed, our memories reshaped and our convictions revised, and yet we speak confidently of the same self enduring through decades. Derek Parfit argued, in a famous and unsettling thesis, that what matters in survival is not strict identity but psychological continuity and connectedness, a matter of degree rather than an all-or-nothing fact. If a teleporter were to destroy me and construct a perfect replica elsewhere, the question whether the replica is really me would, on his view, be empty: there would be no further fact to be right or wrong about. Many readers recoil, yet Parfit insisted that the doctrine was liberating. The walls of the self, he wrote, had seemed like a glass tunnel, and in abandoning the belief in a deep separate ego, he felt less concern for his own future and more for the lives of others. Whether such a consolation is warranted or merely seductive is the sort of question that the ship, still afloat, declines to answer.','Parfit cho rằng điều quan trọng không phải là đồng nhất tuyệt đối mà là sự liên tục tâm lý, và nhận định này khiến ông quan tâm hơn đến người khác.','What is Parfit\'s central claim in the passage?|Identity is absolute and indivisible|*Psychological continuity matters more than strict identity|Teleportation is physically impossible§How did Parfit regard his own doctrine?|*As liberating|As a source of deep despair|As trivial§The phrase "declines to answer" personifies the ship to suggest that|the ship has been destroyed|the author refuses to discuss it|*the puzzle remains unresolved');
RD('C2','Hume and the Sun Tomorrow','Hume và mặt trời ngày mai','☀️','That the sun will rise tomorrow is a proposition on which we would stake nearly anything, and yet David Hume contended that no argument can rationally secure it. Our confidence rests on the experience that it has always risen, but to infer from past regularity to future conduct is to assume that nature is uniform, and that assumption is itself supported only by past experience. The reasoning, in short, is circular. Hume did not advise us to abandon expectation; on the contrary, he held that custom and habit make such inference irresistible and that the sceptic who denied it would starve for lack of conviction. His point was rather that the foundation of our beliefs about the world is psychological rather than logical, a disconcerting concession for an age that prized reason above everything. Later thinkers have sought to evade the difficulty. Popper maintained that science does not rely on induction at all, proceeding instead by bold conjecture and attempted refutation, though critics noted that our trust in theories that have survived refutation looks suspiciously like induction in disguise. The problem has proved admirably resilient: it is not that anyone seriously doubts the dawn, but that no one has produced a justification which does not presuppose what it set out to prove.','Hume chỉ ra rằng suy luận từ quá khứ sang tương lai không thể được biện minh bằng lý trí thuần túy mà dựa trên thói quen.','What is circular about the inference to the future?|It depends on a mathematical error|It ignores the evidence of experience|*It assumes the uniformity of nature, which itself rests on past experience§According to Hume, why do we continue to hold such beliefs?|Logic compels us to do so|*Custom and habit make them irresistible|Scientists have proved them§The word "admirably" in the final sentence conveys|*a wry respect for the problem\'s endurance|sincere approval of Hume|irritation at philosophers');
RD('C2','The Resource Curse Reconsidered','Lời nguyền tài nguyên được xem xét lại','🛢️','It seems perverse that countries blessed with oil or minerals should so frequently be poorer, less democratic and more prone to conflict than their barren neighbours, yet the pattern was sufficiently conspicuous that economists christened it the resource curse. Several mechanisms have been proposed. A sudden export boom appreciates the currency and strangles other tradable sectors, the so-called Dutch disease; revenue that flows directly to the state weakens the bargain between rulers and citizens, since governments that do not tax their people need not heed them; and the volatility of commodity prices wreaks havoc on public budgets. More recent scholarship, however, has complicated the fatalism. Norway and Botswana demonstrate that abundance need not be ruinous where institutions are strong before the windfall arrives, and some researchers suspect that weak institutions are the cause of both resource dependence and poor performance, in which case the curse is a mirage conjured by confounding. This does not render the topic idle. Policymakers have devised sovereign wealth funds, transparent contracts and the direct distribution of revenue to citizens in order to sever the link between windfall and impunity. What the debate teaches is that natural wealth is not destiny but a test, and that the examination is set by institutions rather than by geology.','Tài nguyên thiên nhiên không tự nhiên gây nghèo đói; yếu tố quyết định là thể chế có vững mạnh trước khi nguồn lợi xuất hiện hay không.','What does "Dutch disease" refer to in the passage?|*A currency appreciation that damages other tradable sectors|A disease caused by mining|A tax on commodity exports§What do Norway and Botswana demonstrate?|Resource wealth always leads to ruin|Oil is less harmful than minerals|*Strong prior institutions can prevent the curse§The closing metaphor of a "test" suggests that|natural wealth guarantees success|*outcomes depend on institutional quality, not geology|geologists should write policy');
RD('C2','Hegemony and the Architecture of Bretton Woods','Bá quyền và kiến trúc Bretton Woods','💱','In July 1944, with the war not yet won, delegates from forty-four nations gathered in a New Hampshire hotel to design an economic order that would prevent a repetition of the interwar catastrophe. The arrangement they devised pegged currencies to the dollar, which was in turn convertible into gold, and created the International Monetary Fund and the institution that became the World Bank. Keynes, representing a diminished Britain, argued for a supranational reserve currency and for obliging surplus countries as well as deficit countries to adjust; the American delegation, confident in its pre-eminence, prevailed. The consequence was a system that functioned admirably for a quarter of a century, largely because a single power was willing to underwrite it. Theorists of hegemonic stability infer from this that an open economy requires a benevolent patron, and that the system falters when the patron\'s capacity or resolve wanes. The suspension of gold convertibility in 1971 seemed to confirm the thesis. Sceptics demur, observing that the successor arrangements, though less orderly, have not collapsed, and that cooperation has survived the decline of dominance. The question that remains is whether the earlier harmony was the work of a hegemon or merely the product of a historical moment too exceptional to generalise from.','Hệ thống Bretton Woods hoạt động tốt nhờ vai trò bảo trợ của Mỹ, nhưng liệu cần một bá chủ để duy trì trật tự mở vẫn còn tranh cãi.','What did Keynes advocate at Bretton Woods?|The dollar as the only reserve currency|*A supranational reserve currency and adjustment by surplus countries too|The abolition of the IMF§What do theorists of hegemonic stability infer?|*An open order requires a benevolent patron|Open economies need no leadership|Gold should never be abandoned§The author\'s final stance on the hegemon thesis is|firmly supportive|dismissive and sarcastic|*unresolved, offering an alternative explanation');
RD('C2','Fountain and the Provocation of the Readymade','Fountain và sự khiêu khích của vật có sẵn','🚽','In 1917, a porcelain urinal, signed R. Mutt and tilted onto its back, was submitted to an exhibition in New York and promptly rejected. The gesture is generally attributed to Marcel Duchamp, who thereby inaugurated a question that has pursued the visual arts ever since: what, precisely, makes something a work of art? His answer, delivered with Gallic insouciance, was that the artist\'s choice suffices. The object had not been fashioned by the artist\'s hand, nor did it display any evident beauty; it was the act of selection and the context of display that transformed it. The implications were revolutionary. If craftsmanship was not the criterion, then the sanctity of the unique, skilfully wrought object was dissolved, and attention shifted from the thing to the concept. Admirers regard this as a liberation, and much of what followed, from Pop to conceptualism, can be traced to it. Detractors argue that it opened the gates to charlatanry and left the public uncertain whether it was being instructed or mocked. Both parties may be partly right. A century on, the urinal has acquired a solemnity its maker would doubtless have found hilarious: it is reproduced in textbooks, insured for fortunes and revered in the very institutions it was designed to unsettle.','Duchamp cho thấy hành vi lựa chọn và bối cảnh có thể biến một vật bình thường thành nghệ thuật, mở đường cho nghệ thuật khái niệm.','According to the passage, what made the urinal a work of art in Duchamp\'s view?|Its beauty and fine craftsmanship|Its rejection by the exhibition|*The artist\'s act of selection and the context of display§What did the readymade shift attention towards?|The skill of the maker|*The concept behind the object|The price of the object§The final sentences contain irony because|*the work is now revered by the institutions it meant to unsettle|Duchamp never saw the urinal|museums refuse to display it');
RD('C2','Neorealism and the Dignity of the Ordinary','Chủ nghĩa tân hiện thực và phẩm giá của đời thường','🚲','Italian cinema in the years after the war abandoned the gleaming studios of the previous regime for the rubble of the streets. Neorealism, as the movement came to be called, favoured location shooting, non-professional actors and stories drawn from the plight of the poor. In Vittorio De Sica\'s Bicycle Thieves of 1948, a labourer whose livelihood depends on a stolen bicycle wanders Rome with his young son in a futile search, and the drama arises not from villainy or coincidence but from the sheer fragility of a life lived at the margin. Critics sometimes praised such films as documentary, but the label does them a disservice. The compositions are meticulous, the emotional trajectory carefully modulated, and the final humiliation of the father, who is driven to attempt the very theft that ruined him, is constructed with an almost classical sense of irony. What distinguished the movement was less a rejection of artifice than a redefinition of what was worthy of the camera. By granting an unemployed workman the attention previously reserved for kings and film stars, the neorealists proposed that moral seriousness is not the monopoly of the exalted, and that the ordinary, regarded without condescension, is inexhaustibly dramatic.','Phim tân hiện thực Ý quay ở đời thường với diễn viên không chuyên và cho thấy cuộc sống người nghèo cũng đáng được kể một cách nghiêm túc.','What drives the drama of Bicycle Thieves?|*The fragility of life at the margin|A villain\'s elaborate scheme|A series of lucky coincidences§Why does the author say "documentary" does the films a disservice?|They contain no real locations|They were filmed in studios|*Their composition and emotional design are carefully crafted§The author\'s attitude to the neorealists is|mocking|*appreciative and analytical|indifferent');
RD('C2','Semantic Drift and the Treachery of Words','Trôi nghĩa và sự phản trắc của từ ngữ','📚','Words are not coins of fixed denomination; they wear smooth with handling, and sometimes the face is altogether transformed. The English adjective nice, derived from a Latin term meaning ignorant, has passed through senses signifying foolish, wanton, fastidious and subtle before settling on its present amiable blandness. Such journeys are neither random nor perverse. Linguists recognise recurrent patterns: amelioration, whereby a word ascends in esteem, and pejoration, whereby it declines; broadening, as when a term for a specific object expands to cover a category; and narrowing, as when the reverse occurs. Euphemism is a perpetual engine of change, for the polite substitute inevitably absorbs the stigma of what it replaces and must itself be replaced, a phenomenon sometimes called the euphemism treadmill. Purists who lament that a word is being misused are therefore invoking a fixed standard that history does not supply: the sense they defend was itself, in all likelihood, the usurper of an earlier meaning. This does not make every innovation admirable, and clarity may justify resisting a shift that obliterates a useful distinction. It does suggest that the guardians of a language would be better employed as observers than as legislators, since usage, unlike a statute, is amended daily by everyone.','Nghĩa của từ liên tục biến đổi theo những quy luật nhất định, nên khái niệm dùng từ đúng chuẩn cố định là không có cơ sở lịch sử.','What does the example of "nice" illustrate?|The loss of Latin words|*Dramatic changes of meaning over time|The stability of English adjectives§What is the "euphemism treadmill"?|*Polite words take on the stigma of what they replace and are replaced in turn|A fashion for formal language|A method of teaching vocabulary§The author suggests that guardians of language should act more as|legislators than observers|publishers than teachers|*observers than legislators');
RD('C2','Who Governs the Sky? The Politics of Geoengineering','Ai cai quản bầu trời? Chính trị của kỹ thuật địa cầu','☁️','Solar radiation modification, the proposal to reflect a small fraction of sunlight back to space by injecting reflective particles into the stratosphere, offers an unnerving bargain. It is technically plausible, relatively inexpensive and capable of cooling the planet within a few years, but it would treat a symptom while leaving the cause untouched, and it would have to be maintained for as long as emissions persist or risk a violent rebound. The scientific uncertainties are considerable, yet the governance questions are more formidable still. Because the effects would be unevenly distributed, with changes in rainfall that might favour one region and punish another, any state with sufficient means could in theory alter the climate for all, without the consent of those who would bear the consequences. There is, moreover, the so-called moral hazard: the mere prospect of a technological remedy may blunt the resolve to cut emissions. Advocates of research reply that ignorance is hardly a safeguard, and that it is better to understand an instrument that may be used in desperation than to confront it unprepared. The challenge is to devise legitimate institutions before the temptation to act unilaterally becomes irresistible, a task for which the international order\'s record is not reassuring.','Kỹ thuật phản xạ ánh sáng mặt trời có thể làm mát hành tinh nhưng đặt ra vấn đề quản trị khó hơn cả khó khăn kỹ thuật.','Why is the proposal described as an "unnerving bargain"?|It is both costly and ineffective|It has been banned by treaty|*It is cheap and fast but leaves the cause untouched and requires maintenance§What is the "moral hazard" mentioned?|Researchers might falsify data|*A technological remedy may weaken the will to cut emissions|Particles may harm aircraft§What is the attitude of the final sentence towards international institutions?|*Doubtful about their track record|Confident of their readiness|Indifferent to their role');
RD('C2','Libet and the Pre-Empted Will','Libet và ý chí bị đi trước','⏱️','In the early 1980s, Benjamin Libet asked volunteers to flex a wrist at a moment of their own choosing and to note when they first felt the urge to do so. Electrodes recorded a build-up of neural activity, the readiness potential, that began several hundred milliseconds before the reported moment of decision. The inference seemed unavoidable and was widely broadcast: the brain has already settled the matter before consciousness is informed, and free will is an after-the-fact narrative. Subsequent scrutiny has been less kind to the headline. The timing of a subjective urge is notoriously hard to report, and later work suggested that the readiness potential may reflect background fluctuation in neural noise rather than a specific decision to act. Moreover, a flick of the wrist chosen at random is a thin proxy for the deliberation that attends choosing a career or forgiving an enemy. Philosophers of a compatibilist bent add that free will never required that decisions arise from nowhere; it requires only that they flow from the agent\'s own reasons and values, whatever neural machinery implements them. It would be rash, therefore, to read the experiment as a verdict. It is better regarded as a provocation, one that obliged us to say more carefully what we meant by freedom in the first place.','Thí nghiệm Libet từng bị cho là bác bỏ ý chí tự do, nhưng các phê bình cho thấy kết luận ấy vội vàng.','What did the readiness potential appear to show?|*Neural activity began before the reported urge to act|Volunteers deliberately delayed their choices|Wrist movements are involuntary§Why do compatibilists reject the sweeping inference?|They deny that brains are involved|They believe consciousness is an illusion|*Free will only requires decisions to flow from the agent\'s reasons§The author calls the experiment a "provocation" meaning that it|angered the volunteers|*prompted clearer thinking about freedom|disproved philosophy');
RD('C2','The Drowning Child and the Limits of Demand','Đứa trẻ chết đuối và giới hạn của đòi hỏi đạo đức','🏊','Peter Singer\'s thought experiment is deceptively simple. If you passed a child drowning in a shallow pond, you would wade in at the cost of ruined shoes, and nobody would think you heroic for doing so. Yet children die daily of preventable causes at a distance that, in moral terms, Singer argues, should make no difference. The conclusion, that affluent people are obliged to give until further giving would sacrifice something of comparable importance, is as logically tidy as it is psychologically intolerable. Critics have attacked it from several directions. Some contend that proximity and special relationships legitimately shape our duties, since a morality that treated one\'s own child and a stranger\'s identically would be alien to most of what we value. Others object that the argument demands a life of relentless self-denial, a charge to which Singer has replied, without evident discomfort, that the demand is a feature of the truth rather than a flaw in the argument. What the debate leaves is a nagging awkwardness. We can fault the premises but find it hard to produce a principled account of why the child in the pond should move us and the child in a distant country should not, and the absence of that account is itself instructive.','Lập luận của Singer buộc ta đối mặt với nghĩa vụ giúp người ở xa, và việc không thể giải thích khác biệt đạo đức giữa gần và xa là điều đáng suy ngẫm.','What conclusion does Singer draw from the pond example?|Distance always changes moral duty|*Affluent people should give until further giving costs something comparable|Heroism is a duty§How has Singer responded to the charge that his view is too demanding?|*He has argued the demand reflects the truth|He has softened the conclusion|He has withdrawn the argument§The final sentence implies that|the premises are plainly false|there is a clear answer|*the lack of a principled distinction is revealing');
RD('C2','Do Numbers Exist? The Platonist\'s Predicament','Số có tồn tại không? Thế khó của người theo Plato','🔢','Ask a mathematician whether there are infinitely many primes and she will answer with serene certainty. Ask her what sort of thing a prime is, and the serenity tends to evaporate. Mathematical Platonism holds that numbers and sets inhabit a realm independent of mind and matter, discovered rather than invented, and it accords with the experience of proving a theorem, which feels less like making than finding. The view, however, collides with an epistemological objection associated with Paul Benacerraf: if abstract objects are causally inert and outside space and time, by what faculty could we ever come to know anything about them? Rival positions have their own embarrassments. Formalists, who treat mathematics as a game of symbols, struggle to account for its uncanny applicability to physical reality; intuitionists, who restrict mathematics to what can be mentally constructed, must surrender a great deal of classical analysis. Eugene Wigner spoke of the unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics in the natural sciences, and the phrase has become a standard expression of wonder precisely because no theory dispels it. Perhaps the wisest response is to admit that working mathematicians have little need to resolve the matter, and that the discipline has prospered for centuries in cheerful defiance of its own foundations.','Triết học toán học chưa giải thích được số là gì hay làm sao ta biết về chúng, nhưng toán học vẫn phát triển.','What is the epistemological objection to Platonism?|Numbers are too numerous to count|Mathematicians disagree about theorems|*It is unclear how we could know causally inert abstract objects§What difficulty do formalists face?|Proving any theorem|*Explaining the remarkable applicability of mathematics|Defining the symbols§The final sentence suggests that mathematics|*progresses despite unresolved foundations|has collapsed|will be abandoned until philosophy is settled');
RD('C2','Dark Matter and the Discipline of Doubt','Vật chất tối và kỷ luật của hoài nghi','🔭','Galaxies rotate too quickly. If the visible stars and gas were all that held them together, the outer regions would fly off, yet they remain stubbornly intact, as do clusters of galaxies and the faint patterns imprinted on the early universe. The dominant explanation is that most of the matter in the cosmos is invisible, interacting through gravity but scarcely at all through light. Decades of increasingly sensitive experiments have nonetheless failed to detect a dark matter particle directly, an absence that has emboldened a minority who propose instead that our theory of gravity requires modification at low accelerations. Their proposals reproduce galactic rotation curves with admirable economy but have struggled to account for the larger structures and for the cosmic microwave background without importing invisible ingredients of their own. The history of physics offers precedents in both directions: anomalies in the orbit of Uranus led to the discovery of Neptune, whereas anomalies in the orbit of Mercury required not a new planet but a new theory of gravity. It would be unscientific, then, to declare the matter decided. The prudent attitude is to hold the leading hypothesis firmly enough to work with and loosely enough to abandon, and to remember that the history of science is partly a history of confident entities that failed to appear.','Chuyển động của thiên hà gợi ý về vật chất tối hoặc về việc phải sửa lý thuyết hấp dẫn, và cả hai hướng đều có tiền lệ lịch sử.','What does the Uranus and Mercury comparison show?|*Anomalies may call for either a new entity or a new theory|Planets are easier to find than particles|Gravity theories are always correct§What weakness do modified-gravity proposals have?|They cannot explain any rotation curves|They are too complicated|*They struggle with larger structures and the microwave background§The author recommends that scientists hold the dark matter hypothesis|dogmatically|*firmly but provisionally|with total disbelief');
RD('C2','A Letter to the Translator','Thư gửi người dịch','✉️','My dear Inés, You ask whether you may take liberties with the second chapter, and I confess that my first impulse was to forbid it, which is the impulse of every author who has never had to be read in another tongue. Having reflected, I find that I have no standing to forbid anything. The sentences you find so obstinate were not born in a vacuum; they were shaped by the idiom of a small provincial town that I have not seen in thirty years, and you will perceive, as I do not, how much they depend upon a music that cannot cross the border. Do what the book requires. If the joke about the postmaster is dead in your language, bury it and plant another in the same soil. I would rather be unfaithful to my words than to my intentions, though I realise that this distinction has ruined better friendships than ours. What I ask is only this: that when you hesitate between the elegant rendering and the true one, you remember that the reader you are writing for has never heard my voice and will therefore believe whatever voice you give me. It is a terrible responsibility, and I envy you none of it. With affection and some trepidation, Aurelio.','Một tác giả cho phép dịch giả tự do sáng tạo, miễn là trung thành với ý định hơn là với từng chữ, và ý thức được trách nhiệm nặng nề.','Why does Aurelio change his mind about forbidding liberties?|He no longer cares about the book|*He realises he has no standing to forbid them|He dislikes the second chapter§What does Aurelio mean by planting "another joke in the same soil"?|*Replacing an untranslatable joke with an equivalent in the new language|Writing a new chapter|Ignoring the postmaster entirely§The tone of the letter is best described as|angry and commanding|cold and businesslike|*warm, humble and slightly anxious');
RD('C2','The Dinner at the Vasconcelos House','Bữa tối ở nhà Vasconcelos','🍷','Nobody at the table had mentioned the will, and the omission had acquired the density of furniture. Beatriz poured the wine with the exaggerated care of a woman who wished to be observed pouring it, while her brother examined his plate as though the sole bone of contention had been left there by the cook. Their aunt, who alone among them had nothing to gain and therefore nothing to conceal, talked of the garden, of the swallows that nested under the eaves, of the unforgivable price of figs. It was she who understood, with the merciless clarity of the old, that conversation in such a house is a form of fencing in which the blades are never drawn. When at last Beatriz remarked, as lightly as she could manage, that Father had always been so generous, the silence that followed was of a quality that no one at the table would afterwards be able to describe, though each would remember it for the rest of life. The aunt set down her fork. Generous, she repeated, savouring the word as one might a dubious vintage, and looked from one to the other with an expression of such mild, devastating interest that the candles seemed to gutter.','Trong bữa tối gia đình, vấn đề di chúc bị né tránh cho đến khi một lời nhận xét làm không khí căng thẳng, và người dì nhìn thấu tất cả.','Why does the aunt talk so freely about trivial matters?|She is unaware of the tension|She wants to hide a secret|*She has nothing to gain or hide§What does "conversation... is a form of fencing" imply?|The family practises sport|*People spar indirectly without open confrontation|The talk is polite and friendly§How is the aunt\'s repetition of "generous" best interpreted?|*As a pointed, sceptical challenge|As sincere praise|As a request for the will');
RD('C2','Subsidiarity and the Dilemma of Layered Authority','Nguyên tắc bổ trợ và nan đề của quyền lực nhiều tầng','🏛️','The principle of subsidiarity holds that decisions should be taken at the lowest level of authority competent to take them effectively, and that higher bodies should intervene only when the lower cannot suffice. Rooted in Catholic social teaching, it was embraced by the European Union as a barrier against the centralising tendency that its critics suspected in Brussels. On paper, the principle is elegant, reconciling local self-government with the advantages of scale. In practice, it founders on a deceptively simple question: who decides what the lowest competent level is? If the arbiter is the central institution, the safeguard is compromised; if it is the member states, the Union risks paralysis whenever any government alleges that its prerogatives are threatened. National parliaments have been granted a procedure for registering objections, but the mechanism has been invoked rarely and has seldom altered the outcome. The suspicion lingers that subsidiarity operates less as a binding rule than as a rhetorical resource, invoked sincerely by those who wish to restrain integration and cynically by those who wish merely to obstruct a particular measure. None of this proves the principle empty. A norm need not be enforced to shape expectations, and officials who must justify why action is required at the higher level will at least be compelled to supply reasons.','Nguyên tắc bổ trợ nhằm giữ quyền quyết định ở cấp thấp nhất có năng lực, nhưng việc ai phân xử cấp nào làm nó khó thực thi.','What difficulty does subsidiarity face in practice?|*Deciding who determines the appropriate level of authority|Finding enough national parliaments|Explaining Catholic teaching§What has happened to the objection procedure for national parliaments?|It is used constantly with success|It was abolished|*It has seldom been used and rarely changed outcomes§The author\'s final conclusion about subsidiarity is|that it should be abandoned|*cautiously positive about its influence on reasoning|that it is already fully enforced');
RD('C2','The Panopticon in the Pocket','Panopticon trong túi áo','📱','Jeremy Bentham designed a prison in which a single guard, concealed in a central tower, could observe every cell without ever being seen, the point being that inmates, uncertain at any moment whether they were watched, would discipline themselves. Michel Foucault seized upon the plan as a metaphor for modern power, which operates less through spectacular punishment than through the internalisation of surveillance. The metaphor has aged remarkably well, though its contemporary form differs in instructive ways. The new watchers are not a single tower but a diffuse constellation of platforms, retailers and data brokers, and the observed are not captives but willing customers who trade privacy for convenience with scarcely a murmur. This voluntariness complicates the moral picture. Where Bentham\'s inmates were coerced, today\'s users are enlisted, flattered by personalisation and lulled by the sense that the exchange is trivial. Yet the cumulative effect may be just as disciplinary. Knowing that our searches, movements and associations are recorded, we may subtly curb our curiosity and temper our dissent, even in the absence of any actual penalty. The most insidious feature of such a regime is precisely that it requires no visible authority, only the habit of imagining an audience.','Giám sát hiện đại khác nhà tù của Bentham ở chỗ người dùng tự nguyện, nhưng vẫn có thể khiến ta tự kiềm chế mà không cần quyền lực hữu hình.','How do today\'s watched people differ from Bentham\'s prisoners?|They are better paid|*They are willing participants rather than captives|They are unaware of technology§What effect might constant recording have on users?|*Self-restraint in curiosity and dissent|Greater public protest|Increased trust in platforms§Why is the regime called "insidious"?|It is openly brutal|It is easily detected|*It needs no visible authority, only imagined observers');
RD('C2','Creoles, Pidgins and the Birth of Grammar','Creole, pidgin và sự ra đời của ngữ pháp','🏝️','A pidgin arises when speakers with no common language must improvise a means of communication, typically for trade or labour, and its grammar is minimal, its vocabulary borrowed and its speakers rarely native. What happens when children are born into such a community is among the most arresting episodes in linguistics. Within a generation, the makeshift code can be transformed into a creole: a full language with systematic tense marking, embedded clauses and a stable word order, though no adult ever taught it. Derek Bickerton argued that this reveals an innate bioprogram, a human predisposition to impose grammatical structure on whatever input is available. The thesis is bold and has attracted vigorous opposition, with critics noting that creoles inherit more from their source languages than the theory would predict, and that the historical record of their formation is thin. A similar phenomenon has been documented in the case of Nicaraguan Sign Language, which emerged among deaf children brought together in schools in the 1970s and 1980s and developed grammatical complexity without any model. Such cases bolster the intuition that children are not passive recipients of language but active architects of it, though the extent of their innate endowment remains, as the field has learned to say, an empirical question.','Trẻ em sinh ra trong cộng đồng pidgin có thể biến nó thành ngôn ngữ đầy đủ ngữ pháp, gợi ý xu hướng bẩm sinh về cấu trúc ngôn ngữ.','What distinguishes a creole from a pidgin?|It has fewer speakers|It is used only for trade|*It is a full language with systematic grammar§What do critics say about Bickerton\'s thesis?|Creoles show no grammar at all|*Creoles inherit more from source languages than predicted|Nicaraguan Sign Language is a pidgin§The closing phrase "an empirical question" implies that|*evidence rather than argument must settle the issue|the question is meaningless|the debate is already finished');
RD('C2','The Productivity Puzzle','Câu đố về năng suất','⚙️','Robert Solow quipped in 1987 that one could see the computer age everywhere except in the productivity statistics. Decades on, the remark retains an unsettling relevance. Despite the proliferation of digital technologies, growth in output per hour in advanced economies has been disappointingly sluggish since the middle of the first decade of this century. Explanations compete. Pessimists, like Robert Gordon, contend that the great inventions of the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, electrification, sanitation and the internal combustion engine, were one-off transformations that cannot be repeated by apps. Optimists reply that general-purpose technologies take a generation to diffuse, since firms must reorganise their processes before the new tools bear fruit, as happened when factories took decades to redesign themselves around the electric motor. A third camp suspects the measurement itself: free digital services and improvements in quality are notoriously ill captured in official figures, though most studies find that mismeasurement accounts for only a small portion of the shortfall. The stakes are substantial, for productivity is the source of rising living standards in the long run and the means by which ageing societies might support their pensioners. If the optimists are right, patience will be rewarded; if the pessimists are right, politicians would do well to stop promising what arithmetic will not deliver.','Dù công nghệ số lan rộng, năng suất tăng chậm, và ba cách giải thích cạnh tranh nhau: phát minh cạn kiệt, độ trễ khuếch tán, và sai số đo lường.','What do optimists argue about new technologies?|*Firms need time to reorganise before benefits appear|Technologies are useless|Apps are more important than electricity§What do most studies find about mismeasurement?|It explains the entire shortfall|It is entirely absent|*It explains only a small part of the shortfall§The last sentence is addressed with a tone that is|admiring of politicians|*pointedly cautionary towards politicians|wholly neutral');
RD('C2','History as Narrative and the Problem of Emplotment','Lịch sử như tự sự và vấn đề cốt truyện hóa','🗿','Historians pride themselves on fidelity to the archive, yet no archive speaks in sentences. The past arrives as fragments, a ledger here, a letter there, and the historian must decide which survive into the account and in what order. Hayden White provoked a generation of scholars by arguing that this act of arrangement is not neutral: to present a sequence of events as a tragedy, a comedy or a romance is to impose a literary form that the evidence itself does not dictate. The revolution that appears inevitable in retrospect might, told with a different emphasis, seem a series of accidents. White\'s critics accused him of dissolving the distinction between history and fiction, a charge he rejected, though he never persuasively explained how one might rank rival emplotments by anything other than taste. The more sober lesson is not that history is invented but that it is composed, and that the most trustworthy practitioners are those who make their compositional choices visible. A narrative that advertises its own limits, admitting what the sources cannot reveal and what alternative readings they permit, is likelier to deserve belief than one that flows with the smoothness of destiny. Hindsight, after all, lends the past a coherence it never possessed for those who lived through it.','Cách sắp xếp sự kiện thành cốt truyện không trung lập, nên nhà sử học đáng tin là người bộc lộ những lựa chọn tường thuật của mình.','What was Hayden White\'s main argument?|History consists only of invented stories|*Arranging events into a literary form is not neutral|Archives never contain letters§Which statement best reflects the author\'s view?|*Trustworthy historians reveal their compositional choices|Historical narrative should be abandoned|Smooth narratives are the most reliable§The final sentence implies that retrospective accounts|were unknown to those who lived|are always false|*give the past more coherence than participants experienced');
RD('C2','The Stoic Citadel','Pháo đài của người theo phái Khắc kỷ','🏯','The Meditations of Marcus Aurelius were never intended for publication. They are the private memoranda of an emperor who, between campaigns on the northern frontier, reminded himself of truths he found it all too easy to forget: that he would meet that day with the meddlesome, the ungrateful and the arrogant, that the opinion of others is a poor foundation for self-esteem, and that the obstacle in the path becomes the path. The Stoic doctrine on which he drew distinguishes sharply between what lies within our power, our judgements and intentions, and what does not, namely reputation, health and the conduct of others. Tranquillity, on this account, derives not from mastering circumstances but from declining to be enslaved by them. Popular versions of Stoicism sometimes reduce this to a technique for stoical endurance, a stiff upper lip with a philosophical pedigree. That caricature misses the point that the Stoics demanded active engagement in public life and regarded indifference to outcomes as compatible with rigorous devotion to duty. Marcus governed an empire racked by plague and war, and the poignancy of his book lies in the contrast between the scale of his authority and the modesty of his aim, which was merely to be, in the face of everything, a decent human being.','Marcus Aurelius dùng triết học Khắc kỷ để giữ bình thản và làm tròn bổn phận giữa quyền lực và khủng hoảng, chứ không chỉ là chịu đựng.','Why were the Meditations written, according to the passage?|As public propaganda|As legal instructions for officials|*As private reminders to himself§What is the author\'s objection to the popular version of Stoicism?|It is too complicated|*It reduces Stoicism to mere endurance and ignores engagement in public life|It rejects the idea of duty§What gives the book its poignancy?|*The contrast between his great power and his modest aim|The accuracy of its military records|Its optimistic account of the empire');
RD('C2','The Myth of Westphalia','Huyền thoại về Westphalia','🗺️','Textbooks of international relations habitually date the birth of the modern state system to 1648, when the treaties of Westphalia concluded the Thirty Years\' War and, so the story goes, established the principle that each sovereign is supreme within its borders and may not be interfered with from outside. It is a tidy origin myth, and like most such myths it has been recently and rather mercilessly interrogated. The texts of the settlements say little about sovereignty in the sense now meant; they concern, for the most part, the territorial and confessional arrangements of the Holy Roman Empire and the protection of religious minorities, which, if anything, authorised outside guarantors to intervene. The doctrine of exclusive sovereignty emerged gradually, and its triumph is more properly associated with the legal and diplomatic thought of later centuries. Why, then, has the legend persisted? Partly because it supplies a simple foundation for a discipline that craves one, and partly because it flatters the contemporary assumption that the state is the natural unit of political life. To say this is not to deny that 1648 was significant. It is to suggest that the most influential histories are sometimes those that compress a prolonged evolution into a single date, and that the compression, while convenient, conceals the contingency of what it describes.','Mốc Westphalia 1648 thường bị coi là khởi đầu của chủ quyền nhà nước hiện đại, nhưng thực tế khái niệm này hình thành dần và muộn hơn.','What do the Westphalian treaties mainly address, according to the passage?|*Territorial and religious arrangements within the Holy Roman Empire|The creation of modern nation-states|The invention of diplomacy§Why has the legend persisted, in the author\'s view?|It has been proven by documents|It is taught only in law schools|*It offers a simple foundation and flatters assumptions about the state§The author\'s attitude to the date 1648 is|dismissing it as irrelevant|*acknowledging its importance while questioning its mythologising|celebrating it uncritically');
RD('C2','The Sublime and the Trembling Spectator','Cái cao cả và người xem run rẩy','🏔️','Edmund Burke, in his youthful treatise of 1757, distinguished the beautiful, which is small, smooth and gently inviting, from the sublime, which is vast, obscure and attended by terror. The mountain, the storm and the abyss delight us, he argued, only so long as we are at a safe distance; the pleasure is the peculiar relief of danger contemplated without being undergone. Kant refined the idea, locating the sublime not in the object but in the mind, which, confronted by what exceeds its powers of imagination, discovers in the very failure the superiority of reason and of its own moral vocation. The Romantic painters took this inheritance and ran with it, dwarfing their solitary figures against cliffs and cloud, and the tradition survives in every photograph of a lone hiker facing a glacier. It would be tempting to suppose that modern life, with its satellites and statistics, has rendered the sublime obsolete. The opposite may be true. We now confront quantities, such as the age of the universe or the magnitude of the climate\'s inertia, that no imagination can compass, and the vertigo they induce is recognisably Burke\'s. The difference is that the safe distance is harder to maintain, for the threat that exhilarates in a painting is, in the case of the warming planet, entirely real.','Cái cao cả mang lại khoái cảm khi ta chiêm ngưỡng nguy hiểm từ xa, và trong thời hiện đại nó trở nên đáng lo hơn khi mối đe dọa là có thật.','According to Burke, when do we take pleasure in terrible objects?|When we are directly in danger|*When we are at a safe distance|When they are small and smooth§How does Kant locate the sublime?|*In the mind\'s response to what exceeds imagination|In the mountain itself|In the beauty of nature§Why does the author say the safe distance is "harder to maintain" today?|Paintings no longer exist|Mountains have become smaller|*Some modern threats, like climate change, are real');
RD('C2','Naming an Epoch: The Anthropocene Debate','Đặt tên cho một kỷ nguyên: tranh luận về Anthropocene','🌋','Geologists name epochs after the strata they leave behind, and the proposal that we now live in the Anthropocene, an interval defined by humanity\'s imprint on the planet, has therefore sparked a dispute as much about method as about meaning. Advocates point to a wealth of candidates for a marker in the rock: radioactive isotopes dispersed by atomic tests, plastics, concrete, the bones of billions of domestic chickens, and a shift in the chemistry of ocean sediments. In 2024 the relevant stratigraphic body voted against formalising the epoch, a decision that surprised few who had noticed the unease among its members. Opponents argued that human influence has been gradual and diachronic, with no sharp boundary comparable to the asteroid impact that closed the Cretaceous, and that the concept is more political than geological. Yet the term has outlived the verdict, because it performs work that technical classification cannot. It condenses an intuition that humanity has become a planetary force and invites reflection on responsibility at the scale of deep time. Perhaps the controversy shows that a word can be indispensable for public thought without being admissible in a formal chart, a distinction which scientists, no less than the rest of us, are sometimes reluctant to concede.','Giới địa chất bỏ phiếu không chính thức hóa Anthropocene, nhưng khái niệm này vẫn hữu ích cho tư duy công chúng về trách nhiệm của loài người.','What was the 2024 decision of the stratigraphic body?|It named the epoch officially|It rejected all human influence|*It declined to formalise the Anthropocene epoch§Why did opponents object to a formal boundary?|The evidence was entirely missing|*Human influence was gradual with no sharp marker|Geologists dislike politics§The author suggests that the term Anthropocene|*remains valuable in public discourse despite the vote|should be banned from use|is purely a geological term');
RD('C2','Homecoming at Dusk','Trở về lúc chạng vạng','🚉','He had rehearsed the arrival for twenty years, in rooms where nobody knew the name of the village, and now that the train had deposited him on the platform he discovered that the rehearsals had been wasted. The station was smaller than memory had allowed, the fountain was dry, and the baker\'s shop had become a place that sold telephones. What unsettled him was not the change but its indifference: nothing had been altered in order to punish his absence, and nothing had been preserved in order to welcome him. A woman crossed the square with a bag of oranges and glanced at him with the courteous incuriosity reserved for strangers. It occurred to him that he had been carrying about, all those years, not a place but a photograph, and that the photograph, though faithful, had lacked the one attribute of the living: the capacity to move on without him. He walked slowly towards the church, whose bell struck the hour in a tone that he recognised so exactly that his throat tightened. Some things, it seemed, had waited after all. Whether they had waited for him or merely continued, in their stolid way, to be what they were, he could not tell, and he suspected that the distinction would trouble him for the remainder of his stay.','Người trở về làng sau hai mươi năm nhận ra nơi chốn trong ký ức chỉ là một bức ảnh, và quê hương đã tiếp tục sống mà không có anh.','What unsettles the man most about the village?|*The indifference of the changes to his absence|The hostility of the villagers|The destruction of the church§What does the photograph metaphor suggest?|He had taken many pictures|The village was old-fashioned|*His memory was fixed while the place kept changing§The last sentence reveals that the man is|certain the village welcomed him|*uncertain whether the surviving things were awaiting him|eager to leave immediately');
RD('C2','Address to the Graduating Class','Diễn văn gửi lớp tốt nghiệp','🎓','Graduates, parents, and those faculty who have stayed awake: I have been asked to offer you wisdom, a commodity of which I possess a modest and unreliable supply. What I can offer instead is a report from the other side of the lectern. You have been rewarded, for most of your lives, for being correct. You have been trained to locate the right answer, to submit it before the deadline and to be graded accordingly. The world into which you are about to be released operates on a different economy. There, the questions are ill-posed, the answers contested, and the people who matter most are frequently those who were willing to be wrong in public, and to be wrong again, until they were less wrong than anyone else. I do not say this to console you in advance for your failures, though you will have them in abundance. I say it because the fear of error is the most efficient thief of ambition I have encountered, and it is unusually adept at disguising itself as prudence. Be sceptical of that disguise. Take the job that slightly frightens you, argue with the person you admire, and cultivate, above all, the rare and unfashionable talent of saying, without embarrassment, that you did not know and you have changed your mind.','Diễn giả khuyên sinh viên đừng sợ sai lầm, vì nỗi sợ ấy che giấu dưới vẻ thận trọng và cản trở tham vọng.','What contrast does the speaker draw between school and the world?|School is easy and the world is hard|*School rewards correct answers, while the world rewards willingness to be wrong|School teaches only arguments§What does the speaker mean by fear of error "disguising itself as prudence"?|*Fear can appear to be sensible caution|Prudence is always a mistake|Errors are always prudent§The speaker\'s tone is best described as|bitter and accusing|solemn and distant|*self-deprecating yet earnest');
RD('C2','Can a Machine Be Wronged?','Máy móc có thể bị đối xử bất công không?','🦾','The question of moral status has historically been answered by appeal to some capacity: reason for the Kantians, sentience for the utilitarians, relational standing for those who root ethics in community. Artificial systems unsettle each of these criteria in a different way. A language model may produce fluent testimony of suffering without there being, so far as anyone can establish, anything it is like to be it; the appearance of distress is cheap, and our intuitions, shaped over millennia to respond to faces and voices, are poorly calibrated for entities that can imitate them effortlessly. Sceptics regard the matter as settled: a statistical engine no more suffers than a thermostat feels the cold. Yet the confidence is perhaps premature, since we lack an accepted theory of consciousness from which the exclusion could be deduced. The cost of error is asymmetric and cuts both ways. To attribute moral standing to a mere mechanism wastes sympathy and invites manipulation by those who build persuasive imitations; to withhold it from a genuine subject would replicate, on a grand scale, the historical pattern of denying the inner lives of those inconvenient to acknowledge. The responsible posture may therefore be one of disciplined uncertainty, neither credulous nor contemptuous.','Chưa có lý thuyết ý thức nào cho phép khẳng định máy có hay không có địa vị đạo đức, nên cần thái độ bất định có kỷ luật.','Why are our intuitions poorly calibrated for AI systems?|Humans have never studied machines|Machines refuse to speak|*They evolved to respond to faces and voices, which AI can imitate§What is the author\'s view of the sceptics\' confidence?|Fully justified by science|*Possibly premature, given no accepted theory of consciousness|Dishonest§What does the author mean by "the cost of error is asymmetric and cuts both ways"?|*Both over-attributing and under-attributing standing carry serious risks|Only one mistake is costly|Machines make costly errors');
RD('C2','Memento Mori and the Economy of Attention','Memento mori và nền kinh tế của sự chú ý','⌛','The medieval injunction to remember that one must die was never intended as a counsel of despair. It served, rather, as an instrument of clarification, a means of stripping the trivial from the significant by reminding the living how little time they had to waste upon the former. Our age, which prizes the avoidance of mortality as the only coherent public ambition, has largely abandoned the practice, and the consequences are visible in the way we distribute our attention. A life regarded as indefinitely extensible tolerates the postponement of everything, including the conversation one means to have and the work one means to attempt. The attention economy exploits this complacency with exquisite precision, offering endless diversions to a creature that forgets its finitude. To recover the old discipline does not require morbidity. It requires only the occasional recognition that the number of summers remaining is countable, and that the question what do I want is inseparable from the question what am I prepared to forgo. Those who have been brought close to death often report a curious lucidity: priorities rearrange themselves, grievances shrivel and the ordinary acquires a radiance. The tragedy is that so many of us require a catastrophe in order to see what a well-chosen reminder might have shown us.','Nhắc nhở về cái chết giúp ta phân biệt điều quan trọng với điều vụn vặt, trong khi thời đại hiện nay né tránh nó và lãng phí sự chú ý.','What was the purpose of memento mori, according to the passage?|*To clarify what is significant|To cause despair|To prevent illness§How does the attention economy exploit our attitude to time?|It forces people to work more|It reminds them of death|*It offers endless diversions to those who forget their finitude§What does the final sentence regret?|That reminders are too frequent|*That people often need a catastrophe to gain clarity|That many people die early');
RD('C2','Arrow\'s Theorem and the Arithmetic of Collective Choice','Định lý Arrow và số học của lựa chọn tập thể','🗳️','It is natural to suppose that a sufficiently ingenious voting system could faithfully aggregate the preferences of a population into a single collective ranking. In 1951, Kenneth Arrow demonstrated, with the austere elegance of a mathematician, that this hope is vain. He specified a handful of apparently modest conditions: that the system should handle any combination of individual rankings, that if everyone prefers one option to another so should the group, that the verdict between two options should depend only on how voters rank those two, and that no single voter should be a dictator. Where there are three or more alternatives, he proved, no ranked procedure can satisfy all four at once. The theorem is frequently misread as a proof that democracy is impossible, which it is not; it shows that every method of aggregation must sacrifice something, and that the choice of which compromise to accept is itself political. Defenders of particular systems point out that the pathological cases are rare in practice, and that real electorates tend to hold preferences structured in ways that evade the paradox. That may be so, but the lesson endures. When a result is hailed as the will of the people, one is entitled to inquire which of several possible wills the chosen procedure happened to elicit.','Định lý Arrow chứng minh không có phương pháp bầu cử xếp hạng nào thỏa mãn mọi điều kiện hợp lý, nên mọi hệ thống đều phải đánh đổi.','What does Arrow\'s theorem establish for three or more alternatives?|Democracy is mathematically impossible|*No ranked system can meet all four conditions at once|Dictators are always necessary§What misreading of the theorem does the author correct?|*That it proves democracy is impossible|It applies only to two options|That it was proven in 1951§The final sentence encourages readers to|trust all electoral outcomes|abolish ranked voting immediately|*ask which collective will the procedure actually produced');
